data training_scope_subject_note: String = "THE SUBJECT IS THE JEDEC DDR4 INITIALIZATION CALIBRATION SEQUENCE — which calibration steps a module must execute, derived from its construction. Grounded on JESD79-4A. The load-reduced steps exist only where a data buffer is interposed, which is what makes the step set a difference in KIND between module classes rather than a reliability preference. NOT platform failure receipts (those are gunbc observations of a board), NOT purchasing policy, NOT the DRAM module catalog shape itself (extdeps.memory.types)."

// WHY A TRAINING-STEP MODEL RATHER THAN A BLOCKLIST.
//
// "Avoid 4Rx4 LRDIMM" is a conclusion, and a conclusion authored as a rule
// cannot be re-derived, cannot explain itself to a buyer, and cannot extend to
// a part nobody has tried. What actually happened is mechanical: initialization
// runs a SEQUENCE OF CALIBRATION STEPS, the set of steps depends on the
// module's construction, and a platform that fails one step implicates exactly
// those modules that must execute it. Encoding the steps makes the avoidance
// list a DERIVED consequence, and makes the reason legible at the point of
// purchase.
// Host-to-DRAM PHY calibration. Every module executes these regardless of
// buffering, so a failure here implicates nothing about module class.
// On LrdimmDataBufferMrep:
// Data-buffer calibration. These exist ONLY on load-reduced modules, which
// interpose a buffer between the DRAM and the host. A registered module has
// no data buffer and never executes them — that is why the discriminator
// below is structural rather than statistical.
type MemoryTrainingStep
  = PhyWriteLeveling
  | PhyReadGateLeveling
  | PhyReadLeveling
  | PhyVrefdqCalibration
  | LrdimmDataBufferMrep
  | LrdimmDataBufferWriteLeveling
  | LrdimmDataBufferReadTraining

data phy_training_steps: List<MemoryTrainingStep> = [
  PhyWriteLeveling,
  PhyReadGateLeveling,
  PhyReadLeveling,
  PhyVrefdqCalibration,
]

data lrdimm_data_buffer_training_steps: List<MemoryTrainingStep> = [
  LrdimmDataBufferMrep,
  LrdimmDataBufferWriteLeveling,
  LrdimmDataBufferReadTraining,
]

// The step set a module must complete, DERIVED from its construction. This is
// the single place the RDIMM/LRDIMM distinction becomes a set difference
// rather than a preference.
fn training_steps_required(module: extdeps.memory.types.DramModuleCatalogRow) -> List<MemoryTrainingStep> {
  match module.buffering {
    extdeps.memory.types.LoadReduced => concat(phy_training_steps, lrdimm_data_buffer_training_steps)
    extdeps.memory.types.Registered => phy_training_steps
    extdeps.memory.types.Unbuffered => phy_training_steps
  }
}

// The step's name as the JEDEC/platform literature spells it, for a diagnostic
// that has to say WHICH step. A projection of the closed coproduct, so it lives
// beside the coproduct rather than being re-spelled by each consumer that needs
// to name a step in a refusal.
fn memory_training_step_label(step: MemoryTrainingStep) -> std.types.NonEmptyStr {
  match step {
    PhyWriteLeveling => "PHY write leveling" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
    PhyReadGateLeveling => "PHY read gate leveling" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
    PhyReadLeveling => "PHY read leveling" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
    PhyVrefdqCalibration => "PHY VREFDQ calibration" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
    LrdimmDataBufferMrep => "LRDIMM data-buffer read-phase calibration (MREP)" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
    LrdimmDataBufferWriteLeveling => "LRDIMM data-buffer write leveling" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
    LrdimmDataBufferReadTraining => "LRDIMM data-buffer read training" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn module_executes_step(module: extdeps.memory.types.DramModuleCatalogRow, step: MemoryTrainingStep) -> Bool {
  any(training_steps_required(module: module), s => s == step)
}

// CAPACITY FORCES TOPOLOGY, and this is derived from JEDEC die density rather
// than asserted from market observation. A module's required die density is
// capacity / (data chips per rank * ranks). When the density a simple low-rank
// module would need exceeds the largest monolithic die the generation defines,
// that capacity is UNREACHABLE without adding ranks or stacking — which is
// precisely how 64GB DDR4 came to exist as quad-rank LRDIMM before 16Gbit dies
// shipped, and why the CHEAPEST 64GB DDR4 parts are disproportionately the
// load-reduced ones. The buyer's intuition that a lower speed grade is simply
// a discount is therefore wrong at capacities near the density ceiling: the
// older part is not the same module clocked slower, it is a different topology.
// MonolithicDie in the constructed row below is load-bearing, not a default carried over from
// the input. This builds the COUNTERFACTUAL plain module -- the one that would exist if the
// capacity were reachable without the two escapes the comment above names, extra ranks and
// stacking. Propagating module.die_stacking would ask "is this capacity reachable given that it
// is already stacked", which is trivially yes and answers nothing.
fn hypothetical_module_at_rank_count(
  module: extdeps.memory.types.DramModuleCatalogRow,
  rank_count: Int,
) -> extdeps.memory.types.DramModuleCatalogRow {
  extdeps.memory.types.DramModuleCatalogRow {
    part_number: module.part_number,
    manufacturer: module.manufacturer,
    generation: module.generation,
    capacity_bytes: module.capacity_bytes,
    data_rate_mts: module.data_rate_mts,
    ecc: module.ecc,
    buffering: extdeps.memory.types.Registered,
    die_stacking: extdeps.memory.types.MonolithicDie,
    chip_width: module.chip_width,
    rank_count: rank_count,
  }
}

// Can this capacity be built as an ordinary low-rank registered module in this
// generation, or does reaching it REQUIRE extra ranks or die stacking?
fn capacity_reachable_as_dual_rank_registered(
  module: extdeps.memory.types.DramModuleCatalogRow,
  jedec: extdeps.memory.types.JedecDdrGenerationCatalogRow,
) -> Bool {
  extdeps.memory.types.dram_module_is_realizable(
    module: hypothetical_module_at_rank_count(module: module, rank_count: 2),
    jedec: jedec,
  )
}

data memory_training_step_note: String = "The step sets here are the structural claim this model rests on: a load-reduced module interposes a data buffer between the DRAM and the host, so it must calibrate that buffer, and a registered module has no such buffer and therefore cannot fail a buffer-calibration step. That is a difference in KIND, not in reliability, which is what makes it usable as a purchase filter rather than as a probability. It is also why the same failure reproduced on two different modules of one family and never on the registered parts the host ran previously. MREP is the DDR4 data-buffer read-phase calibration step; it is named explicitly because it is the step observed failing on the ALTRAD8UD-1L2T platform, both on gunbc's own hosts and in an independent public report carrying byte-identical controller status words. The PHY steps are listed for completeness and to keep the model honest in the other direction: a PHY-step failure would implicate every module class equally and would NOT support a buffering-based purchase rule."
